[{"id":3,"slug":"a-fence-a-lighthouse-and-a-fog-bell","link":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/samples/slack-tide/a-fence-a-lighthouse-and-a-fog-bell/","date_gmt":"2026-10-06T09:00:00","title":{"rendered":"A Fence, a Lighthouse, and a Fog Bell"},"content":{"rendered":"<p>This letter is about a fence. It stands at the end of a short breakwater on a lake I know well, and for three summers I walked past it without a second look. The posts are grey, split along the grain, and leaning the way things lean after sixty years of doing one job. Behind them stands a small white lighthouse. Most people photograph the lighthouse. I started photographing the fence, and I have not been able to stop.</p>\n<p>The fence keeps the shingle from walking into the channel. That is all it does. Each post was set by hand, and each one has been replaced at some point, so the line is a patchwork of different decades. Newer posts are paler, with the saw marks still sharp. Older ones are rounded off by wind and salt.</p>\n<p>The fog bell is the other half of this letter. It sits on a short iron frame beside the lighthouse. On a clear day it hangs silent. On the morning I visited, the fog came in so fast that the first strike startled me. One low note, then nothing, then another. By the fortieth strike I had stopped counting and started listening, which I suspect is the point.</p>\n<p>That is the letter. Next time I will be on the flats at low water. If you have a fence, a bell, or a post with a story near you, write back and tell me about it. I read every reply.</p>\n<p><em>Photograph: Holly's Coastal View, via Wikimedia Commons.</em></p>"},"excerpt":{"rendered":"Sixty years of posts at the end of a breakwater, and a bell that only rings in fog."},"author":2,"categories":[1],"tags":[],"_embedded":{"wp:featuredmedia":[{"source_url":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/demo/slack-tide/h-fence.jpg"}],"author":[{"id":2,"name":"Slack Tide","slug":"slacktide"}]}},{"id":4,"slug":"walking-the-flats-at-low-water","link":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/samples/slack-tide/walking-the-flats-at-low-water/","date_gmt":"2026-09-08T09:00:00","title":{"rendered":"Walking the Flats at Low Water"},"content":{"rendered":"<p>This month is a cove at Martin National Wildlife Refuge, photographed by the U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service. At low water a place like this stops being sea and becomes ground you can almost walk on.</p>\n<p>I like a photograph taken at the bottom of the tide. Everything that is usually hidden is out in the open for an hour, and then it is gone again.</p>\n<p><em>Photograph: U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service, Northeast Region, via Wikimedia Commons.</em></p>"},"excerpt":{"rendered":"A cove at Martin National Wildlife Refuge, when the tide is out and the mud has the say."},"author":2,"categories":[1],"tags":[],"_embedded":{"wp:featuredmedia":[{"source_url":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/demo/slack-tide/h-flats.jpg"}],"author":[{"id":2,"name":"Slack Tide","slug":"slacktide"}]}},{"id":5,"slug":"dunlins","link":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/samples/slack-tide/dunlins/","date_gmt":"2026-08-04T09:00:00","title":{"rendered":"Dunlins"},"content":{"rendered":"<p>Dunlins at Prime Hook National Wildlife Refuge in Delaware, from the U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service. A whole letter about small brown birds is a risk, but here we are.</p>\n<p>Shorebirds move as one body. You see the flock before you see a single bird, and when it turns the colour of it changes. That is the whole letter this month: go and watch one turn.</p>\n<p><em>Photograph: USFWS Headquarters, via Wikimedia Commons.</em></p>"},"excerpt":{"rendered":"A flock at Prime Hook in Delaware, and why I keep a pair of binoculars by the door."},"author":2,"categories":[1],"tags":[],"_embedded":{"wp:featuredmedia":[{"source_url":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/demo/slack-tide/h-dunlins.jpg"}],"author":[{"id":2,"name":"Slack Tide","slug":"slacktide"}]}},{"id":6,"slug":"the-island-lighthouse-in-spring","link":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/samples/slack-tide/the-island-lighthouse-in-spring/","date_gmt":"2026-07-07T09:00:00","title":{"rendered":"The Island Lighthouse in Spring"},"content":{"rendered":"<p>The lighthouse on Anacapa Island with wildflowers in front of it, photographed by Robert Schwemmer for NOAA. A light usually gets photographed against bad weather. This one gets a spring hillside.</p>\n<p>I keep this picture as a reminder that the coast has a good season too, and that it is short.</p>\n<p><em>Photograph: Robert Schwemmer, CINMS, NOAA, via Wikimedia Commons.</em></p>"},"excerpt":{"rendered":"Anacapa Island, its light, and a hillside of wildflowers."},"author":2,"categories":[1],"tags":[],"_embedded":{"wp:featuredmedia":[{"source_url":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/demo/slack-tide/h-anacapa.jpg"}],"author":[{"id":2,"name":"Slack Tide","slug":"slacktide"}]}},{"id":7,"slug":"standing-where-the-swell-breaks","link":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/samples/slack-tide/standing-where-the-swell-breaks/","date_gmt":"2026-06-02T09:00:00","title":{"rendered":"Standing Where the Swell Breaks"},"content":{"rendered":"<p>A stretch of the Big Island coast from the U.S. Geological Survey. Dark rock, white water, nothing else in the frame.</p>\n<p>Geologists photograph a coast to record it, not to make it pretty, and I think that is why their pictures age so well. There is nothing in them to go out of fashion.</p>\n<p><em>Photograph: U.S. Geological Survey, via Wikimedia Commons.</em></p>"},"excerpt":{"rendered":"The coast of the Big Island of Hawaii, seen by the U.S. Geological Survey."},"author":2,"categories":[1],"tags":[],"_embedded":{"wp:featuredmedia":[{"source_url":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/demo/slack-tide/h-surf.jpg"}],"author":[{"id":2,"name":"Slack Tide","slug":"slacktide"}]}},{"id":8,"slug":"rock-pools","link":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/samples/slack-tide/rock-pools/","date_gmt":"2026-05-05T09:00:00","title":{"rendered":"Rock Pools"},"content":{"rendered":"<p>A tide pool at Yehliu Geopark in Taiwan, photographed in April 2019 and shared by Balon Greyjoy. A rock pool is the sea kept in a cup for a few hours.</p>\n<p>If you have one near you, sit by it for ten minutes before you look for anything. It takes that long for the pool to forget you are there.</p>\n<p><em>Photograph: Balon Greyjoy, via Wikimedia Commons.</em></p>"},"excerpt":{"rendered":"A tide pool at Yehliu on the north coast of Taiwan."},"author":2,"categories":[1],"tags":[],"_embedded":{"wp:featuredmedia":[{"source_url":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/demo/slack-tide/h-tidepool.jpg"}],"author":[{"id":2,"name":"Slack Tide","slug":"slacktide"}]}},{"id":9,"slug":"a-marsh-seen-from-above","link":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/samples/slack-tide/a-marsh-seen-from-above/","date_gmt":"2026-03-03T09:00:00","title":{"rendered":"A Marsh Seen From Above"},"content":{"rendered":"<p>An aerial view of the coastal marsh at Wertheim National Wildlife Refuge, taken in 2016 by the U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service. From above, a marsh looks like handwriting.</p>\n<p>I have never seen a marsh from the air myself. I have walked the edge of a few. This is the view I would ask for if anyone offered me a seat.</p>\n<p><em>Photograph: U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service, Northeast Region, via Wikimedia Commons.</em></p>"},"excerpt":{"rendered":"Wertheim National Wildlife Refuge from the air, where the water draws its own map."},"author":2,"categories":[1],"tags":[],"_embedded":{"wp:featuredmedia":[{"source_url":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/demo/slack-tide/h-marsh.jpg"}],"author":[{"id":2,"name":"Slack Tide","slug":"slacktide"}]}},{"id":10,"slug":"cork-floats-and-old-nets","link":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/samples/slack-tide/cork-floats-and-old-nets/","date_gmt":"2026-01-13T09:00:00","title":{"rendered":"Cork Floats and Old Nets"},"content":{"rendered":"<p>A fishing net at Karystos on Euboea in Greece, photographed by Jebulon. Nets in harbours are always either being mended or waiting to be.</p>\n<p>This is the first letter of the year, so it is a short one. The net is the picture. The rest of the year is the mending.</p>\n<p><em>Photograph: Jebulon, via Wikimedia Commons.</em></p>"},"excerpt":{"rendered":"A fishing net drying at Karystos, on the Greek island of Euboea."},"author":2,"categories":[1],"tags":[],"_embedded":{"wp:featuredmedia":[{"source_url":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/demo/slack-tide/h-floats.jpg"}],"author":[{"id":2,"name":"Slack Tide","slug":"slacktide"}]}},{"id":11,"slug":"the-ferry-arch-on-a-slow-tuesday","link":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/samples/slack-tide/the-ferry-arch-on-a-slow-tuesday/","date_gmt":"2025-11-04T09:00:00","title":{"rendered":"The Ferry Arch on a Slow Tuesday"},"content":{"rendered":"<p>The Pier 43 Ferry Arch in San Francisco, photographed by Daderot. An arch built for crowds looks different on a quiet day.</p>\n<p>I like harbour buildings that outlive their timetable. They stand there like a sentence that has lost the rest of its paragraph, and you get to guess the ending.</p>\n<p><em>Photograph: Daderot, via Wikimedia Commons.</em></p>"},"excerpt":{"rendered":"The Pier 43 Ferry Arch in San Francisco, with nobody waiting under it."},"author":2,"categories":[1],"tags":[],"_embedded":{"wp:featuredmedia":[{"source_url":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/demo/slack-tide/h-arch.jpg"}],"author":[{"id":2,"name":"Slack Tide","slug":"slacktide"}]}},{"id":12,"slug":"point-reyes-in-a-westerly","link":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/samples/slack-tide/point-reyes-in-a-westerly/","date_gmt":"2025-09-02T09:00:00","title":{"rendered":"Point Reyes in a Westerly"},"content":{"rendered":"<p>The Point Reyes lighthouse in California, from the NOAA photo library, credited to the Common Murre Restoration Project of the U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service.</p>\n<p>A lighthouse is a building whose only job is to be seen. This one does it from a ledge, which seems to me the honest way.</p>\n<p><em>Photograph: Common Murre Restoration Project, USFWS, via Wikimedia Commons.</em></p>"},"excerpt":{"rendered":"The Point Reyes lighthouse, from the NOAA photo library."},"author":2,"categories":[1],"tags":[],"_embedded":{"wp:featuredmedia":[{"source_url":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/demo/slack-tide/h-reyes.jpg"}],"author":[{"id":2,"name":"Slack Tide","slug":"slacktide"}]}},{"id":13,"slug":"spruce-over-grey-water","link":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/samples/slack-tide/spruce-over-grey-water/","date_gmt":"2025-07-08T09:00:00","title":{"rendered":"Spruce Over Grey Water"},"content":{"rendered":"<p>The shoreline at Huron National Wildlife Refuge, from the U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service. Trees right down to the water and a lake that might as well be a sea.</p>\n<p>Not every coast is salt. Some of my favourite water has no tide at all and still behaves like it does.</p>\n<p><em>Photograph: U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service, Midwest Region, via Wikimedia Commons.</em></p>"},"excerpt":{"rendered":"The shoreline at Huron National Wildlife Refuge on Lake Superior."},"author":2,"categories":[1],"tags":[],"_embedded":{"wp:featuredmedia":[{"source_url":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/demo/slack-tide/h-cliff.jpg"}],"author":[{"id":2,"name":"Slack Tide","slug":"slacktide"}]}},{"id":14,"slug":"the-channel-at-muskegon","link":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/samples/slack-tide/the-channel-at-muskegon/","date_gmt":"2025-06-03T09:00:00","title":{"rendered":"The Channel at Muskegon"},"content":{"rendered":"<p>The channel and lighthouse at Muskegon, Michigan, photographed by NOAA’s Great Lakes Environmental Research Laboratory.</p>\n<p>A channel is a promise made in concrete: this way out, this way back. I find that comforting in a picture and slightly less so on a boat.</p>\n<p><em>Photograph: NOAA Great Lakes Environmental Research Laboratory, via Wikimedia Commons.</em></p>"},"excerpt":{"rendered":"The Muskegon channel and its light, from NOAA’s Great Lakes lab."},"author":2,"categories":[1],"tags":[],"_embedded":{"wp:featuredmedia":[{"source_url":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/demo/slack-tide/h-channel.jpg"}],"author":[{"id":2,"name":"Slack Tide","slug":"slacktide"}]}},{"id":15,"slug":"a-harbour-postcard-eighty-years-on","link":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/samples/slack-tide/a-harbour-postcard-eighty-years-on/","date_gmt":"2025-04-01T09:00:00","title":{"rendered":"A Harbour Postcard, Eighty Years On"},"content":{"rendered":"<p>Fishing boats in the harbour at Boothbay Harbor, Maine, on a postcard published by Tichnor Brothers. The colours are the printer’s, not the sea’s.</p>\n<p>Old postcards are the best record of what a town wanted you to remember. The boats got the front of the card. Everything else got the back.</p>\n<p><em>Photograph: Tichnor Brothers, publisher, via Wikimedia Commons.</em></p>"},"excerpt":{"rendered":"Fishing boats at Boothbay Harbor, Maine, on an old Tichnor Brothers card."},"author":2,"categories":[1],"tags":[],"_embedded":{"wp:featuredmedia":[{"source_url":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/demo/slack-tide/h-boothbay.jpg"}],"author":[{"id":2,"name":"Slack Tide","slug":"slacktide"}]}},{"id":16,"slug":"the-sea-cave-at-marica","link":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/samples/slack-tide/the-sea-cave-at-marica/","date_gmt":"2025-03-04T09:00:00","title":{"rendered":"The Sea Cave at Maricá"},"content":{"rendered":"<p>The Gruta da Sacristia, a sea cave in Maricá in the state of Rio de Janeiro, photographed by Wilfredor.</p>\n<p>A sea cave is the slowest kind of building work there is. This letter is short because the cave took long enough for both of us.</p>\n<p><em>Photograph: Wilfredor, via Wikimedia Commons.</em></p>"},"excerpt":{"rendered":"The Gruta da Sacristia in Maricá, Rio de Janeiro."},"author":2,"categories":[1],"tags":[],"_embedded":{"wp:featuredmedia":[{"source_url":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/demo/slack-tide/h-grotto.jpg"}],"author":[{"id":2,"name":"Slack Tide","slug":"slacktide"}]}},{"id":17,"slug":"lichen-on-the-seaward-side","link":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/samples/slack-tide/lichen-on-the-seaward-side/","date_gmt":"2025-02-04T09:00:00","title":{"rendered":"Lichen on the Seaward Side"},"content":{"rendered":"<p>The shoreline of Lighthouse Island at Huron National Wildlife Refuge, from the U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service.</p>\n<p>Look at the rock before the water. The colour on the seaward side is the part that has been paying attention the longest.</p>\n<p><em>Photograph: U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service, Midwest Region, via Wikimedia Commons.</em></p>"},"excerpt":{"rendered":"The shore of Lighthouse Island at Huron National Wildlife Refuge."},"author":2,"categories":[1],"tags":[],"_embedded":{"wp:featuredmedia":[{"source_url":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/demo/slack-tide/h-rock.jpg"}],"author":[{"id":2,"name":"Slack Tide","slug":"slacktide"}]}},{"id":18,"slug":"two-sails-leaving-sunset-key","link":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/samples/slack-tide/two-sails-leaving-sunset-key/","date_gmt":"2025-01-07T09:00:00","title":{"rendered":"Two Sails Leaving Sunset Key"},"content":{"rendered":"<p>Sailboats off Sunset Key in Florida, photographed in 2025 by Julian Lupyan. A first letter should start with something leaving harbour, so this one does.</p>\n<p>Slack Tide is a letter about coasts, once a month or so, one picture and a few paragraphs. Thank you for being here at the start.</p>\n<p><em>Photograph: Julian Lupyan, via Wikimedia Commons.</em></p>"},"excerpt":{"rendered":"The first letter: two boats off Sunset Key in Florida."},"author":2,"categories":[1],"tags":[],"_embedded":{"wp:featuredmedia":[{"source_url":"https://keyturnkit.github.io/demo/slack-tide/h-sailboat.jpg"}],"author":[{"id":2,"name":"Slack Tide","slug":"slacktide"}]}}]